    relation: "app/Actions/Application/StopApplicationOneServer.php:15 `if ($application->destination->server->isSwarm()) {` in `handle(Application $application, Server $server)` (def at :13) - the machine to act on arrives as the second argument, but the eligibility question is asked of a DIFFERENT machine: the thing's main target, walked out of its deployment target, and a swarm answer there returns early at :16 and stops nothing anywhere; the containers are then found and killed on the passed machine at :22 and :29-:35"

  # -- the machine is derived twice over, for a source and a target, and the two are compared
  - id: d:clone-source-target
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/CloneMe.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/CloneMe.php:209 `$sourceServer = $database->destination->server;` with `$targetServer = $newDatabase->destination->server;` at :210, handed to `VolumeCloneJob::dispatch($sourceVolume, $targetVolume, $sourceServer, $targetServer, $newPersistentVolume);` at :212 - neither machine is chosen here by name: both are walked out of the old and the new thing's deployment targets, and the same three lines repeat for the two service shapes at :340-:343 and :395-:398; the machine the operator actually picked on this screen is only an integer (:35) resolved at :90"
  - id: d:volume-clone-same-machine
    group: dependents
    match: [VolumeCloneJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/VolumeCloneJob.php:33 `if (! $this->targetServer || $this->targetServer->id === $this->sourceServer->id) {` in `handle()` (def at :30) - the receiving end of that pair, and the whole strategy turns on it: same machine (or no target at all, the nullable at :24) copies the volume locally at :34, different machines streams it between two machines at :36; the branch is decided by comparing the two derived machines, so a change in how a thing is attached silently reroutes data over the network or fails to"

  # -- the machine is not used at all: it is COUNTED, and the count decides whether the action
  # -- is allowed
  - id: d:team-server-quota
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/Team.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Team.php:116 `$servers = $team->servers->count();` in `public static function serverLimitReached(?Team $team = null)` (def at :109), compared against the plan's allowance at :115 and returned at :118 `return $servers >= $serverLimit;` - a gate that decides whether another machine may exist at all, and the sibling `serverOverflow()` at :130 asks the inverse at :132; no machine is touched here, only how many there are, and every add-a-machine path in the product is gated on it"
  - id: d:key-in-use-guard
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/PrivateKey.php]
    relation: "app/Models/PrivateKey.php:328 `return $this->servers()->exists()` as the FIRST clause of `isInUse()` (def at :326), or-ed with three other kinds at :329-:331 and consumed by `safeDelete()` at :336 `if (! $this->isInUse())` - a key may not be deleted while any machine still uses it, so the existence of an attachment, and not any machine, is what blocks the action; the check never loads a machine and never names one"
  - id: d:token-in-use-guard
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/CloudProviderToken.php]
    relation: "app/Models/CloudProviderToken.php:39 `return $this->servers()->exists();` in `public function hasServers(): bool` (def at :37), over the relation declared at :32-:35 - the same shape one level out: whether a provider credential is still attached to any machine decides whether the operator may remove it, and the answer is an existence query with no machine in hand"

  # -- the machine set is fetched for a screen, and where it is fetched from decides what the
  # -- operator can act on
  - id: d:logs-ownership-check
    group: dependents
    match: [Shared/GetLogs.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/GetLogs.php:127 `if (! Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->where('id', $this->server->id)->exists()) {` opening `getLogs($refresh = false)` (def at :125) - before any log is streamed the machine this component holds is re-checked against the set the current team owns, and a miss returns 'Unauthorized.' at :128; the eligibility question is asked of a SET, not of the machine, so which set that query returns decides what an operator is allowed to see"
  - id: d:shared-vars-cached-set
    group: dependents
    match: [SharedVariables/Server/Index.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/SharedVariables/Server/Index.php:15 `$this->servers = Server::ownedByCurrentTeamCached();` in `mount()` (def at :13), into the `public Collection $servers` declared at :11 - the machines whose shared variables an operator can edit come out of a CACHED set rather than a live query, so the screen's population is whatever that cache last held; nothing on the line says which machines those are or when the set was built"
